{"qid":"Q1071439","names":["Merina Kingdom"],"title":"Merina Kingdom","endonym":{"native":"Fanjakan’i Madagasikara","lang":"mg"},"capsule":"The Merina Kingdom was the dominant pre-colonial state of Madagascar, originating in the central Highlands around the late sixteenth century and expanding under Andrianampoinimerina (r. c. 1787–1810) from a reunified Imerina into an expansionist power. His son Radama I (r. 1810–1828) allied with Britain, accepted British officers to modernize his army, and extended Merina sovereignty over approximately two-thirds of Madagascar by the late 1820s — the first time most of the island had been under a single authority. The kingdom developed a written administrative system using a romanized script introduced by the London Missionary Society, and Antananarivo grew into a substantial capital. France invaded in 1883 and again in 1895, and in 1897 the French parliament voted to annex Madagascar as a colony, deposing and exiling the last queen Ranavalona III and abolishing the monarchy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Merina Kingdom's core highland territory and the areas of direct conquest under Radama I are reasonably well-attested through European missionary and British diplomatic records, but exact boundaries of tributary versus directly administered zones are imprecise.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Merina Kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merina_Kingdom"},{"label":"Britannica: Merina","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Merina"},{"label":"Britannica: Madagascar history","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Madagascar/History"}],"b